          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "needs-review",
          "text": "Description: On July 21, 2013, around 4 a.m., a witness is intrigued by the fixed presence of a dark red light in the cloudy sky. The witness's observation direction (Courcelles) corresponds to an azimuth of 230°. The witness describes a round, red object four times larger than the Moon, at an altitude of 25°, and notes that the sky was cloudy, with no visible stars. It is observed that the Moon is precisely at an azimuth of 231°, at an altitude of 4.6°. It is highly likely that the witness fell victim to the classic illusion of the Moon, which always appears very large at sunrise or sunset. Partially obscured by a cloud veil, the Moon did not reveal its surface details, which would have allowed the witness to identify it. After a midnight awakening, it is common for rare phenomena to greatly surprise witnesses. The GEIPAN classifies this case as \"B\": probable Moon observation (the significant deviation in the phenomenon's altitude in the sky justifies this case not being classified as \"A\").",
          "translation_text_sha256": "03da7813d31ba2fff3466dc49b8c3e2f9db1aeb8110046cad0c079207d83c830"
        }
